a risk of poor  reproducibility  then another issue and that is  particularly pertinent to animal  research  is the use of small sample sizes and of  course  usually we do all we can in order to use  as few animals  as possible in order not to  waste animals unnecessarily  but of course if you're not using enough  animals then  this may mean that you lack statistical  power to detect the effect that you're  looking for  and so in the end your results may be  meaningless simply because you have not  used  enough animals then another big issue  is analytical flexibility in many cases  and that is certainly true  for a lot of animal research  studies contain or or include subgroups  of animals  different sexes different age groups  different treatments  and often a number of different outcome  variables are assessed and so there is a  lot of flexibility in statistical  analysis  and of course if you explore the data  and look for statistically significant  associations  and then present these as if they have  been  the result of a rigorously designed  confirmatory study  then you may overblow  your conclusions and you may you may  conclude things from spurious  findings and the final point i want to  mention here is publication bias  or selective reporting in general so if  several outcome measures are assessed  but then only the ones that turn out to  be statistically significant are  published  or whole studies are not published if  they do not  yield statistically significant results  then this is obviously going to distort  the evidence that is published in the  literature and so we cannot rely on  what has been published if a lot of  a lot of results that did not show up as  statistically significant  have simply been put under the carpet  now i'm not going to talk about  these things any further but instead  what i'm going to focus on  is the issue of rigorous standardization  and this is something that is not  usually considered as  a source of poor reproducibility but i'm  trying to convince you in the course of  this presentation  that this is actually a major issue in  animal research  now first look let's look into what  rigorous standardization means  standardization essentially means the  defining of the characteristics of the  animals in their environment  and to keep these characteristics  constant  and of course this leads to a  homogeneous  study sample that is studied under  rigorously controlled and standardized  conditions  and the important point i want to make  here is that  if we compare it to the other sources of  poor reproducibility  they are all considered to be bad  practice they are actually violations of  good research practice  but in terms of rigorous standardization  this is actually something  that is considered as good practice  and many textbooks of labor to animal  science  they actually recommend  to use rigorous and the intention behind  this  is of course to get results that are as  precise as possible  and by reducing the variation in the  results  to be able to use fewer animals for the  same study  now if we want to understand  why this could potentially be a problem  for reproducibility  we first need to go back in history by  about 350 years  to the birth of reproducibility as a  scientific principle to establish  matters of fact back in the 17th century  the royal society at some point decided  that in order to distinguish between  anecdotes reported by  scientists and robust scientific  evidence that they need  some kind of quality control and the  measure that they took  in order to do this was that they  required from then  on that in order for a result to be  accepted as scientific evidence  it needed to be replicated by an  independent  laboratory so at that time  in order to for a result to be accepted  as scientific evidence  it was not sufficient to just publish a  single observation or a single study  but the study had to be replicated  independently  by an independent scientist  now of course since that time we've come  a long way  and these days as you all know we don't  need to  plan multi-laboratory studies or  independent replications in order to get  funding  and we don't need to report independent  replications in order to get our results  published  instead what we're doing is instead of  just observing one animals  we build replicates into our  study design so the picture here  illustrates  uh animals waiting for a study and  instead of just absorb  observing one animal or one cage of  animals  we have a sample size a sample of  animals that we're studying and these  animals are  counting as independent replicates  within  our study design now the problem with  this  is of course that the animals within  such a study population they are a lot  less  independent of each other than an  independent observation in an  independent laboratory  by independent scientists and in  particular  if we come back to the rigorous  standardization that is happening in  laboratory animal science  these animals are usually identical  usually genetically identical they may  be inbred strains or even clones  they have the same age they have been  fed the same food they are housed under  the same conditions  and they are cared for by the same care  stuff and in the end tested  by the same experimenter so they have  lots  of factors that shape their phenotype in  common  and of course the more these conditions  are standardized within the laboratory  the more they risk to be different  from the conditions in a different  laboratory  and the first time that this  got actually the attention  of scientists or in the scientific  community  was a paper published in 1999  by three behavioral geneticists who got  together  to test um different inbred strains of  mice  uh the eight uh strains that are listed  here on this  on this figure and compared them in a  number of different behavioral tests  as is usually done when new mutants  are behaviorally characterized  or when these animals are used in order  to screen  for the effects of new drugs or new  mutations  so as you can see in this picture um  across the three laboratories i've  highlighted  two strains in color and as you can see  uh on this particular outcome measure  an outcome measure of a conventional  anxiety test the elevated plasmas test  you can see that the difference between  these two strains  in albany goes in one direction  in portland it goes in the opposite  direction and in  edmonton there is no difference at all  between these two  strains of mice so  i've put the term despite here in  inverted commas so  poor reproducibility despite rigorous  standardization meaning that people were  discussing well how could this happen  how can results be so  different if because these scientists  they tried  to do as much as was possible to  standardize  the conditions not just the test  conditions but also the housing  conditions  of these animals across the three  laboratories and yet  they sometimes found completely  different results  uh between different strains there were  other cases  um if you look at it on at the two  strains here on the very left you can  see that  there is a little bit of variation  between the different laps  but in general the difference between  the two strains goes in the same  direction  in all three labs and there is a big  difference between these two strains  across  all three laboratories now the problem  is  that in order to know whether you have  such a robust  strain difference or a difference that  may be spurious and may be  limited to the specific conditions of  your own laboratory  in order to know that you need to vary  conditions  it doesn't mean that you need to do a  multi-laboratory study as here  but at least you need to introduce some  variation  in your study design in order to assess  whether a difference is a robust effect  or is very specific to the specific  standardized conditions  that were used in in that experiment  and this is what i have referred to as  the standardization fallacy  namely the attempt to increase  reproducibility  at the expense of external validity  and this is actually what happens when  conditions are standardized within study  that is that there are of course  many factors that can be standardized  across laboratories  housing conditions food the type of  animal being used  but there are also many factors that  simply cannot be standardized  the microbial biome of these animals  will be  laboratory specific the personnel  interacting with the animals cannot be  standardized and so there will be many  environmental factors that cannot be  standardized but will still  affect the animals in one way or another  and therefore  produce different phenotypes between  different laboratories and of course the  more you standardize the more rigorously  you standardize the conditions within  your study  the more the higher the risk that the  results that you will obtain  will be limited or the validity of these  results will be limited to these  specific conditions  now i was by no means the first to  mention this  and if we go back in history a little  bit you can see that ronald fisher  one of the godfathers of experimental  design and statistics  mentioned this already back in the 30s  of the last century  namely that standardization weakens  rather than strengthens our ground for  inferring a result  and this is because a standardized  experiment can supply  direct information only in respect to  the narrow range of conditions achieved  by standardization  so standardization reduces the inference  space  the space to which we can draw  inferences from the results that we have  obtained  and then of course as is normally the  case when experiments get replicated in  a different lab by independent  scientists the conditions are different  and therefore  if the results aren't robust they will  not be  reproducible  and it is important when we're talking  about animal research to understand  that the variation in our studies are  not just random noise they're not just  measurement error but there is a lot of  biological variation biological  variation is the very essence  of all living organisms so animals  differ  by their genes we know different strains  of mice they have different  characteristics  they differ by sex they differ  depending on which environment they have  been brought up in  so early experience the rearing  conditions  weaning age all that can have a strong  effect on the phenotype of the animals  and the same applies to housing and  husbandry conditions and of course  also the test conditions under which  conditions the animals are tested  will affect the outcome of a study  so what we're talking about here is gene  by environment interactions and of  course  every animal is the product of  the interaction between its genes and  its environmental history  and this leads us to the reproducibility  paradox and i've drawn a simple sketch  here  uh taken from a paper that we've  published a couple of years back  where we try to put  the reproducibility of animal research  into perspective  with biological variation  so what i've drawn here is a so-called  norm of reaction  a change in the reaction of the animals  to a particular stimulus  in response to a particular  environmental parameter  parameter this parameter x can be  temperature  it can be noise it can also be a  treatment as it that is applied  to the animals and and as you can see on  the left side by the distribution of the  trade values  depending on um which  level of the environment parameter i  select i may get  different results now we can translate  this  into replicate study so one lab does a  study  under its own specific standardized  conditions  and obtains a result maybe the dark blue  result and then a different lab tries to  replicate  this study but because the conditions in  that lab are different  they will find a slightly different  result here represented by light blue  so this led us to suggest  that if we want to obtain robust results  that are actually generalizable  across the conditions by which  laboratories vary  and therefore reproducible we need to  take this variation as it exists between  laboratories into account  and so we predicted that if instead of a  single laboratory study under highly  standardized conditions  we would conduct a multi-laboratory  condition that  includes or incorporates all this  variation between laboratories  we would obtain a less precise results  as represented by the wider distribution  of this blue distribution here  but the results should be  more consistent and therefore better  reproducible  across different laboratories  and in order to study this we were using  real data  and this is an example of the data that  we use this  is data of 222 studies on the effect of  hypothermia  on the infarct volume in animal models  of stroke  now you may wonder why was this  experiment conducted 220 times  of course it was not all independent  experiments but because hypothermia  is one of the very few treatments that  are also effective in the clinical  population  against stroke or show an improvement in  the outcome of stroke  hypothermia is used as a positive  control  in many many animal studies of stroke  so we had a lot of data to play with and  they are ranked here  along the effect size that they produced  the gray bar indicates that on average  hypothermia reduces  um the infarct volume  of of a stroke by about  50 so what we did with these data we  selected  50 of these 222 studies  um we select the 50 studies that were  most independent of each other meaning  that they came from different labs and  from different scientists  we then used the parameter estimates as  they were reported in the papers  the mean and the standard deviation in  order to generate  sample values and with these data we  then run simulations  with different sample sizes in some  cases  we use sample sizes of 12 in others  24 or 48 animals per group  samples were taken proportionally from  either one  two three or four studies in order to  mimic  single lab studies and also multi-lab  studies including two  three or four laboratories now i'm going  to show you this  in an example here so here you can see  the the design for a single laboratory  study and for a multi-laboratory study  the multi-laboratory studies includes  three laboratories  so in order to generate a  multi-laboratory study we randomly  picked  three papers from the stack of 50  studies  and then took the data as they were  described in these  papers and then proportionally sampled  from these  three different papers in order to  generate  a multi-lab study comprising in this  case  24 animals so 12 each per  treatment group control and treatment  and compared this to a single laboratory  study  in which case we randomly selected one  study  and then sampled all 24 animals from  this particular  single study  so here are results these are  15 studies of  single lab study simulation so this is  15  independent studies taken from these 50  studies  and then data sampled from  the distribution as described in these  papers and as you can see the results  vary quite considerably  across these 15 studies the red line  is the null line so meaning that there  is no effect of hypothermia  and as you can see 50 or 100 the full  spectrum  and even negative results are  represented in this sample of 15 studies  within this selection of 50 studies that  we looked at  the dotted line is the summary effect  size across all 50 studies so that is  the  roughly 50 percent reduction in infarct  volume  that you get when you do a meta-analysis  across all 50 studies  we then looked for the proportion of  studies that found a significant  effect and an effect that accurately  represents that summary effect  and by significant we mean that the  confidence interval doesn't overlap with  the zero line  and with accurate we mean that the  confidence interval of the study  overlaps  with the summary effect size so as you  can see  from these 50 single lab studies there  were only three  that yielded significant and accurate  effect size estimates now when we  when we look at multi-lab simulations  you can see that already with  simulations based on only two studies  we get much more consistent results and  this  improves even further when we take four  laps into account so when we simulate  the study across four  different laboratories as you can see on  the right side of this slide  the number of results that are  significant  and accurate estimates of the overall  effect size  increases from 3 to 10 out of the 15  when we include two laps or two studies  into  into the simulation and if we include  four laps  then on average we get 14 out of 15  of these multi-lab studies producing  significant and accurate effect size  estimates  and it is important to note that these  multi-lab studies  they include exactly the same number of  data points as the single lab studies so  it's not a matter of sample size  it's simply a matter of um sampling  across a wider distribution of data  as it occurs when experiments are  conducted across multiple labs  we replicated this analysis for 13  different interventions in animal models  of stroke  myocardial infarction and breast cancer  and in all cases except for very small  sample sizes in a few cases  we can see a similar pattern of  improvement  of the reproducibility or the  consistency of the results  with an increasing number of  laboratories included  in the multi-lab study so whereas  in most cases single lab studies  produced significant and and accurate  effect size estimates of in less than 50  percent of the cases  this number increased to um  60 to 80 percent in in  multi-lab studies including three or  four laboratories  now one of the reasons that may explain  this effect is the  heterogeneity that exists between  studies study heterogeneity here  represented as i  square over the 13  different data sets that we analyzed  represents the proportion of variation  that is accounted for  by the laboratory or by the study rather  than the treatment effect that was  studied  and as you can see is that the the  large proportion and in in some cases  almost a hundred percent and in one case  even a hundred percent of the variation  in the data was accounted for by  differences  between studies rather than differences  uh  within studies so differences between  animals within studies  and this is a huge heterogeneity which  of course can be explained by the high  degree of standardization within studies  and a much lower degree of  standardization between  studies and when we compare this  with human clinical trials you can see  that this is a completely different  pattern  even though there are in human clinical  trials there are  studies where you find large  heterogeneities of 60 to 100  the proportion of studies in which you  find much lower values of heterogeneity  is much higher in clinical trials  and we believe that one of the most  important reasons for this  is that of course the human clinical  population  is much more variable in each of the  studies  and is much less standardized within  studies compared to different studies  and therefore  these clinical trials they cover a much  larger proportion of the variation that  is that exists across different studies  compared to animal studies where we  usually have a very narrow  space a very narrow inference space  within studies  and differences between studies this is  also shown  um by a recent study where um  scientists interested in the metabolic  rate and the response to obesity and  laboratory mice were analyzing  a big data set across many different  laboratories  they included nearly 10 000 black six  mice so  all mice inbred mice have the same  strain  but what they found was that institution  so the laboratory where the study was  conducted  had the strongest effect on the  variation in the data  so again an indication that  differences between laboratories can be  massive  and may dominate the results of animal  experiments and if we don't take this  into account  we may produce very precise but rather  spurious findings that do not generalize  even across the conditions  that exist between different  laboratories  so to sum this up we  need to be clear that standardized  single lab studies  produce highly unreal unreliable results  because they usually  uh have a poor external validity due to  the fact  that the inference base is reduced by  uh the high degree of standardization  and that therefore standardization can  be an important  cause of poor reproducibility and what  we propose is that instead of  standardizing study populations  we should actively heterogenize study  populations  in order to improve the reproducibility  and this can be done without a need for  more animals  if we use advanced statistical designs  advanced experimental designs and  statistical methods  so eliminating biological variation  through standardization  is a highly inefficient strategy for  generating scientific evidence because  it means  that each specific genotype by  environment interaction will be  studied in a separate experiment and  this is akin to the atomization of  animal research  and as such each of those very specific  studies will contribute very little  to our information gain  so what are the solutions uh possible  solutions  of course we can go back as  proposed by the royal society in the  17th century  and do an independent replication  this can be a useful strategy  but of course even though it is a simple  strategy it  is a rather inefficient strategy if we  think that  every single experiment would have to be  replicated independent  uh in a different laboratory by other  scientists  and there are certainly better um  solutions to this  one 