ctors  factors of housing conditions husbandry  protocols or test conditions  or by variation of genetic factors  so varying the species to strain or the  sex of the animals  and randomized block designs are the  perfect  design that can be used for this and in  many  animal experiments a blocking design  is appropriate anyway because for  example  several animals are housed in a cage and  if you want to account for  the um the dependence of animals that  are housed within a cage  you would include cage as a blocking  factor in this  in the statistical design anyway now  once you do this  and you have a blocking factor in your  study design there is no reason why you  should not make these blocks more  different between each other  and instead of having all the same  animals in all cages  you may have one strain in one cage and  a different strain in another cage  and some of the animals may be exposed  to more stressful conditions and other  animals may be exposed to less stressful  conditions  and by this you create the range of  genetic and environmental variation  that may or should mimic the variation  that normally exists between  different laboratories and through this  get  results that may be less precise but  will be more robust and have a  higher likelihood to produce  reproducible results  than rigorously standardized study  samples  so with this i would like to thank  here some of my collaborators who  contributed to the data that i've shown  here  in particular bernhard felkel who  contributed a lot both  empirically as well as theoretically and  then  my former phd students lucille fought  and helena richter  and my long-standing collaborator joseph  garner from stanford  and other collaborators that have  contributed to this work in particular  johaf benjamini and nirikafkafi from tel  aviv  and emily sena from edinburgh and on the  right side you see  all the institutions that have supported  me  and my team with funding for this  research  thank yo