terized  or when these animals are used in order  to screen  for the effects of new drugs or new  mutations  so as you can see in this picture um  across the three laboratories i've  highlighted  two strains in color and as you can see  uh on this particular outcome measure  an outcome measure of a conventional  anxiety test the elevated plasmas test  you can see that the difference between  these two strains  in albany goes in one direction  in portland it goes in the opposite  direction and in  edmonton there is no difference at all  between these two  strains of mice so  i've put the term despite here in  inverted commas so  poor reproducibility despite rigorous  standardization meaning that people were  discussing well how could this happen  how can results be so  different if because these scientists  they tried  to do as much as was possible to  standardize  the conditions not just the test  conditions but also the housing  conditions  of these animals across the three  laboratories and yet  they sometimes found completely  different results  uh between different strains there were  other cases  um if you look at it on at the two  strains here on the very left you can  see that  there is a little bit of variation  between the different laps  but in general the difference between  the two strains goes in the same  direction  in all three labs and there is a big  difference between these two strains  across  all three laboratories now the problem  is  that in order to know whether you have  such a robust  strain difference or a difference that  may be spurious and may be  limited to the specific conditions of  your own laboratory  in order to know that you need to vary  conditions  it doesn't mean that you need to do a  multi-laboratory study as here  but at least you need to introduce some  variation  in your study design in order to assess  whether a difference is a robust effect  or is very specific to the specific  standardized conditions  that were used in in that experiment  and this is what i have referred to as  the standardization fallacy  namely the attempt to increase  reproducibility  at the expense of external validity  and this is actually what happens when  conditions are standardized within study  that is that there are of course  many factors that can be standardized  across laboratories  housing conditions food the type of  animal being used  but there are also many factors that  simply cannot be standardized  the microbial biome of these animals  will be  laboratory specific the personnel  interacting with the animals cannot be  standardized and so there will be many  environmental factors that cannot be  standardized but will still  affect the animals in one way or another  and therefore  produce different phenotypes between  different laboratories and of course the  more you standardize the more rigorously  you standardize the conditions within  your study  the more the higher the risk that the  results that you will obtain  will be limited or the validity of these  results will be limited to these  specific conditions  now i was by no means the first to  mention this  and if we go back in history a little  bit you can see that ronald fisher  one of the godfathers of experimental  design and statistics  mentioned this already back in the 30s  of the last century  namely that standardization weakens  rather than strengthens our ground for  inferring a result  and this is because a standardized  experiment can supply  direct information only in respect to  the narrow range of conditions achieved  by standardization  so standardization reduces the inference  space  the space to which we can draw  inferences from the results that we have  obtained  and then of course as is normally the  case when experiments get replicated in  a different lab by independent  scientists the conditions are different  and therefore  if the results aren't robust they will  not be  reproducible  and it is important when we're talking  about animal research to understand  that the variation in our studies are  not just random noise they're not just  measurement error but there is a lot of  biological variation biological  variation is the very essence  of all living organisms so animals  differ  by their genes we know different strains  of mice they have different  characteristics  they differ by sex they differ  depending on which environment they have  been brought up in  so early experience the rearing  conditions  weaning age all that can have a strong  effect on the phenotype of the animals  and the same applies to housing and  husbandry conditions and of course  also the test conditions under which  conditions the animals are tested  will affect the outcome of a study  so what we're talking about here is gene  by environment interactions and of  course  every animal is the product of  the interaction between its genes and  its environmental history  and this leads us to the reproducibility  paradox and i've drawn a simple sketch  here  uh taken from a paper that we've  published a couple of years back  where we try to put  the reproducibility of animal research  into perspective  with biological variation  so what i've drawn here is a so-called  norm of reaction  a change in the reaction of the animals  to a particular stimulus  in response to a particular  environmental parameter  parameter this parameter x can be  temperature  it can be noise it can also be a  treatment as it that is applied  to the animals and and as you can see on  the left side by the distribution of the  trade values  depending on um which  level of the environment parameter i  select i may get  different results now we can translate  this  into replicate study so one lab does a  study  under its own specific standardized  conditions  and obtains a result maybe the dark blue  result and then a different lab tries to  replicate  this study but because the conditions in  that lab are different  they will find a slightly different  result here represented by light blue  so this led us to suggest  that if we want to obtain robust results  that are actually generalizable  across the conditions by which  laboratories vary  and therefore reproducible we need to  take this variation as it exists between  laboratories into account  and so we predicted that if instead of a  single laboratory study under highly  standardized conditions  we would conduct a multi-laboratory  condition that  includes or incorporates all this  variation between laboratories  we would obtain a less precise results  as represented by the wider distribution  of this blue distribution here  but the results should be  more consistent and therefore better  reproducible  across different laboratories  and in order to study this we were using  real data  and this is an example of the data that  we use this  is data of 222 studies on the effect of  hypothermia  on the infarct volume in animal models  of stroke  now you may wonder why was this  experiment conducted 220 times  of course it was not all independent  experiments but because hypothermia  is one of the very few treatments that  are also effective in the clinical  population  against stroke or show an improvement in  the outcome of stroke  hypothermia is used as a positive  control  in many many animal studies of stroke  so we had a lot of data to play with and  they are ranked here  along the effect size that they produced  the gray bar indicates that on average  hypothermia reduces  um the infarct volume  of of a stroke by about  50 so what we did with these data we  selected  50 of these 222 studies  um we select the 50 studies that were  most independent of each other meaning  that they came from different labs and  from different scientists  we then used the parameter estimates as  they were reported in the papers  the mean and the standard deviation in  order to generate  sample values and with these data we  then run simulations  with different sample sizes in some  cases  we use sample sizes of 12 in others  24 or 48 animals per group  samples were taken proportionally from  either one  two three or four studies in order to  mimic  single lab studies and also multi-lab  studies including two  three or four laboratories now i'm going  to show you this  in an example here so here you can see  the the design for a single laboratory  study and for a multi-laboratory study  the multi-laboratory studies includes  three laboratories  so in order to generate a  multi-laboratory study we randomly  picked  three papers from the stack of 50  studies  and then took the data as they were  described in these  papers and then proportionally sampled  from these  three different papers in order to  generate  a multi-lab study comprising in this  case  24 animals so 12 each per  treatment group control and treatment  and compared this to a single laboratory  study  in which case we randomly selected one  study  and then sampled all 24 animals from  this particular  single study  so here are results these are  15 studies of  single lab study simulation so this is  15  independent studies taken from these 50  studies  and then data sampled from  the distribution as described in these  papers and as you can see the results  vary quite considerably  across these 15 studies the red line  is the null line so meaning that there  is no effect of hypothermia  and as you can see 50 or 100 the full  spectrum  and even negative results are  represented in this sample of 15 studies  within this selection of 50 studies that  we looked at  the dotted line is the summary effect  size across all 50 studies so that is  the  roughly 50 percent reduction in infarct  volume  that you get when you do a meta-analysis  across all 50 studies  we then looked for the proportion of  studies that found a significant  effect and an effect that accurately  represents that summary effect  and by significant we mean that the  confidence interval doesn't overlap with  the zero line  and with accurate we mean that the  confidence interval of the study  overlaps  with the summary effect size so as you  can see  from these 50 single lab studies there  were only three  that yielded significant and accurate  effect size estimates now when we  when we look at multi-lab simulations  you can see that already with  simulations based on only two studies  we get much more consistent results and  this  improves even further when we take four  laps into account so when we simulate  the study across four  different laboratories as you can see on  the right side of this slide  the number of results that are  significant  and accurate estimates of the overall  effect size  increases from 3 to 10 out of the 15  when we include two laps or two studies  into  into the simulation and if we include  four laps  then on average we get 14 out of 15  of these multi-lab studies producing  significant and accurate effect size  estimates  and it is important to note that these  multi-lab studies  they include exactly the same number of  data points as the single lab studies so  it's not a matter of sample size  it's simply a matter of um sampling  across a wider distribution of data  as it occurs when experiments are  conducted across multiple labs  we replicated this analysis for 13  different interventions in animal models  of stroke  myocardial infarction and breast cancer  and in all cases except for very small  sample sizes in a few cases  we can see a similar pattern of  improvement  of the reproducibility or the  consistency of the results  with an increasing number of  laboratories included  in the multi-lab study so whereas  in most cases single lab studies  produced significant and and accurate  effect size estimates of in less than 50  percent of the cases  this number increased to um  60 to 80 percent in in  multi-lab studies including three or  four laboratories  now one of the reasons that may explain  this effect is the  heterogeneity that exists between  studies study heterogeneity here  represented as i  square over the 13  different data sets that we analyzed  represents the proportion of variation  that is accounted for  by the laboratory or by the study rather  than the treatment effect that was  studied  and as you can see is that the the  large proportion and in in some cases  almost a hundred percent and in one case  even a hundred percent of the variation  in the data was accounted for by  differences  between studies rather than differences  uh  within studies so differences between  animals within studies  and this is a huge heterogeneity which  of course can be explained by the high  degree of standardization within studies  and a much lower degree of  standardization between  studies and when we compare this  with human clinical trials you can see  that this is a completely different  pattern  even though there are in human clinical  trials there are  studies where you find large  heterogeneities of 60 to 100  the proportion of studies in which you  find much lower values of heterogeneity  is much higher in clinical trials  and we believe that one of the most  important reasons for this  is that of course the human clinical  population  is much more variable in each of the  studies  and is much less standardized within  studies compared to different studies  and therefore  these clinical trials they cover a much  larger proportion of the variation that  is that exists across different studies  compared to animal studies where we  usually have a very narrow  space a very narrow inference space  within studies  and differences between studies this is  also shown  um by a recent study where um  scientists interested in the metabolic  rate and the response to obesity and  laboratory mice were analyzing  a big data set across many different  laboratories  they included nearly 10 000 black six  mice so  all mice inbred mice have the same  strain  but what they found was that institution  so the laboratory where the study was  conducted  had the strongest effect on the  variation in the data  so again an indication that  differences between laboratories can be  massive  and may dominate the results of animal  experiments and if we don't take this  into account  we may produce very precise but rather  spurious findings that do not generalize  even across the conditions  that exist between different  laboratories  so to sum this up we  need to be clear that standardized  single lab studies  produce highly unreal unreliable results  because they usually  uh have a poor external validity due to  the fact  that the inference base is reduced by  uh the high degree of standardization  and that therefore standardization can  be an important  cause of poor reproducibility and what  we propose is that instead of  standardizing study populations  we should actively heterogenize study  populations  in order to improve the reproducibility  and this can be done without a need for  more animals  if we use advanced statistical designs  advanced experimental designs and  statistical methods  so eliminating biological variation  through standardization  is a highly inefficient strategy for  generating scientific evidence because  it means  that each specific genotype by  environment interaction will be  studied in a separate experiment and  this is akin to the atomization of  animal research  and as such each of those very specific  studies will contribute very little  to our information gain  so what are the solutions uh possible  solutions  of course we can go back as  proposed by the royal society in the  17th century  and do an independent replication  this can be a useful strategy  but of course even though it is a simple  strategy it  is a rather inefficient strategy if we  think that  every single experiment would have to be  replicated independent  uh in a different laboratory by other  scientists  and there are certainly better um  solutions to this  one solution is multi-laboratory studies  as i've just shown you  multi-laboratory studies are effective  but they are logistically highly  demanding  it needs a lot of organization in order  to bring different laboratories together  and organize the study in a way that the  conditions  can be compared across laboratories and  so this is something that you don't want  to do for every single experiment but  maybe a solution for a late stage  pre-clinical study  before you decide to go into a clinical  trial perhaps  then another possibility is simply to  adjust  the metrics of significance or the  metrics that you apply in order to  decide whether or not your treatment had  an effect  and this is something that can be  applied to research areas that have  highly harmonized methods  as for example the phenotyping of mouse  mutants  where a lot of independent laboratories  are doing similar types of studies using  similar types of tests  and similar animals and in these cases  it is possible to  simply adjust the p-values of single lab  studies in a way  that takes um the random effect  of labs into account so if you have a  good estimate of  how to what extent results vary across  laboratories  you can take this into account  statistically and just  and adjust your single lab results by  this factor  and finally and that's this is the  method that we are working on most  is uh instead of heterogenizing instead  of standardizing study  uh populations to actively heterogenize  them  in single lab studies so instead of  running a multi-lab study  to mimic a multi-lab study by  producing sub-cohorts within a single  study population a sub-cohort of animals  this can be different strains of animals  that are included or it can be different  housing conditions or different test  conditions that are included in a study  design  here is an illustration heterogenization  of study samples in single lab studies  can either be done by varying  systematically varying environmental  factors  factors of housing conditions husbandry  protocols or test conditions  or by variation of genetic factors  so varying the species to strain or the  sex of the animals  and randomized block designs are the  perfect  design that can be used for this and in  many  animal experiments a blocking design  is appropriate anyway because for  example  several animals are housed in a cage and  if you want to account for  the um the dependence of animals that  are housed within a cage  you would include cage as a blocking  factor in this  in the statistical design anyway now  once you do this  and you have a blocking factor in your  study design there is no reason why you  should not make these blocks more  different between each other  and instead of having all the same  animals in all cages  you may have one strain in one cage and  a different strain in another cage  and some of the animals may be exposed  to more stressful conditions and other  animals may be exposed to less stressful  conditions  and by this you create the range of  genetic and environmental variation  that may or should mimic the variation  that normally exists between  different laboratories and through this  get  results that may be less precise but  will be more robust and have a  higher likelihood to produce  reproducible results  than rigorously standardized study  samples  so with this i would like to thank  here some of my collaborators who  contributed to the data that i've shown  here  in particular bernhard felkel who  contributed a lot both  empirically as well as theoretically and  then  my former phd students lucille fought  and helena richter  and my long-standing collaborator joseph  garner from stanford  and other collaborators that have  contributed to this work in particular  johaf benjamini and nirikafkafi from tel  aviv  and emily sena from edinburgh and on the  right side you see  all the institutions that have supported  me  and my team with funding for this  research  thank you very much uh for your  attention  and of course i'm ready to answer  questions  we cannot have a live chat here but  you're welcome to address your questions  via email and i'll do my best to answer  all of them  as quickly as possible  all righty great job that is a wrap so  we're all set  and pretty much from here i'll just do  some quick editing on the  front and back ends just to get it all  ready for the  event yep  all right um can i do this  i mean can i can i see this um once  you've  you've done it or how do you proceed  with this  definitely after i'm done editing i can  send you a  preview link and you can take a look at  your presentation  using that preview link okay so i can  definitely send that over to you once  it's ready  and then we can go from there with any  feedback you might have  okay thank you very much all righty any  other  questions for me before we wrap up here  watch  oh no no you were totally fine yeah that  was great  yeah awesome well that is  everything do you have any other  questions for me before we wrap up  no that's fine sounds good we'll have a  good rest of your day  and i will talk to you soon okay thank  you very much  bye-bye
 

